From: Christopher Stenger <chris.h.stenger@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] media: cedrus: scale down into the V4L2 capture buffer

Stage 1 of the only no-GPU route to 1080p on this 1280x720 panel.  The VE's
secondary output can halve or quarter each axis at decode time, which is how
the vendor plays 1080p on this SoC -- it downscales at the DECODER, not in the
display pipeline, which is why an exhaustive census of the display blocks found
nothing that could do it.

Patch 0097 proved the hardware works but left the scaled frame in a private
debugfs buffer, where nothing could reach it.  This makes it the V4L2 capture
buffer, so the existing zero-copy dma-buf path to DECD carries it to the panel
unchanged.  1920x1080 decodes to 960x544; drm/h713-afbd then magnifies that
back to the panel.

Userspace asks for it with V4L2_SEL_TGT_COMPOSE on the capture queue, which is
the standard way a decoder advertises scaling.  Each axis divides by 1, 2 or 4
and nothing between -- there is no arbitrary ratio on this part, and the
register block that would provide one is present, accepts every write, and has
no datapath behind it.  A request is rounded down to the next reachable size
and read back, rather than refused.  Offered for H.264 only, because
VE_H264_SDROT_* live inside the H.264 engine block.

THE RECONSTRUCTION CANNOT LIVE IN THE CAPTURE BUFFER ANY MORE.

That is the whole difficulty.  The capture buffer now holds a scaled picture,
which is not something the decoder can predict from, so every reference frame
has to be somewhere else.  Each capture buffer gets a private full-size frame,
and the primary output plus every DPB pointer aim at that instead:

  - all frame addresses now go through one helper, cedrus_frame_addr().  Both
    call sites -- the DPB list and the current picture -- already shared
    cedrus_fill_ref_pic(), and the other codecs share cedrus_dst_buf_addr(), so
    there is a single place where this can be got right or wrong.
  - the frames are per capture buffer rather than a shared pool, because the
    DPB indexes by capture buffer.  Two references alive at once are two
    distinct frames; sharing one would corrupt prediction in a way that still
    decodes and still looks like video.
  - cedrus_dst_format_set() is handed recon_fmt while scaling, so the primary
    output's stride and chroma offset describe the full-size frame.  Handing it
    the now-smaller dst_fmt would put the chroma plane in the wrong place.

Failure here does not look like failure.  It looks like plausible corruption,
so bisect against the known-good MD5s in tools/video/va-decode-test.sh before
trusting any output.

The secondary output is programmed after the codec's setup, because that is
where cedrus_engine_enable() writes VE_MODE and a disabled engine takes the
control word while silently dropping the addresses -- they read back as zero,
which is what made a full 16-value sweep look like absent silicon.  The
addresses go in raw and unshifted, and linear NV12 needs BOTH the EXT table in
VE_CHROMA_BUF_LEN[31:30] and the format in VE_PRIMARY_OUT_FMT[3:0]; miss either
and the tiled default renders as horizontal stripes from a scaler that is
working perfectly.  The driver warns once if the luma address reads back zero.

Compile-tested only.  Nothing here has run on the board yet.

Origin: https://github.com/cstenger/allwinner-h713-mainline/blob/43b2cdd4aa1b161a281db8b10f02ce70085e2d42/patches/kernel/0099-media-cedrus-scale-down-into-the-capture-buffer.patch (0099-media-cedrus-scale-down-into-the-capture-buffer.patch, commit fe65505)
